    "description": "The Zand dynasty governed much of western and southern Iran between 1751 and 1794, with Shiraz its best-known political center. Its founder, Karim Khan, used the title wakil rather than shah, while his successors lost power through internal conflict and Qajar expansion. [s1]",
    "tags": ["Zand dynasty", "Karim Khan", "Shiraz", "Persian Gulf trade", "Eighteenth-century Iran"],
    "status": "Historical ruling dynasty; rule ended in 1794",
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T13:45:59Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T13:45:59Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Zand Dynasty: Karim Khan and Shiraz | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Zand government, trade and architecture under Karim Khan, and the succession struggles that ended with Qajar victory in 1794."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "formation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A regional settlement after Nader",
        "content": "The Zands rose amid the struggles following Nader Shah’s assassination. Karim Khan belonged to a pastoral community associated with the Lak branch of the Lors in western Iran. His rise depended on tribal military support and alliances with urban leaders, rather than inheritance of a stable imperial government. [s1]\n\nAn alliance occupied Isfahan in 1750 and installed a Safavid prince as Ismail III. Karim subsequently defeated his partner Ali Mardan Khan and adopted the title wakil, or deputy. He never formally became shah. His authority extended over much of western and southern Iran, while Khorasan remained outside his rule. Thus Zand and Afsharid histories overlap geographically and chronologically. [s1]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "government-and-recovery",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The deputy’s government",
        "content": "Karim’s reputation for humane government needs to be read alongside the concentrated character of his authority. John R. Perry’s study describes ministers with little independent executive influence and military commands entrusted predominantly to close relatives. Personal accessibility did not amount to representative government. [s2]\n\nEconomic recovery involved practical interventions. Karim encouraged displaced Armenians and Jews to return, supported grain relief during famine, and sought to revive commerce. His dealings with the British East India Company, a commercial corporation with expanding political power in Asia, included concern about silver leaving Iran to pay for imports. Commercial cooperation therefore involved bargaining over resources as well as opening markets. [s2]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "shiraz-and-gulf",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Shiraz and the limits of peace",
        "content": "Shiraz became the setting for Karim’s building program. His citadel, mosque, gardens, and covered bazaar connected government, urban commerce, and architectural patronage. Later destruction and redevelopment altered that ensemble, so the surviving city should not be mistaken for an untouched eighteenth-century capital. [s2]\n\nThe period also included warfare beyond Iran’s southern coast. Zand forces besieged Basra, then an Ottoman port, beginning in 1775 and occupied it after its fall until Karim’s death in 1779. The history of the dynasty consequently combines recovery in its core territories with coercion and conflict elsewhere. [s2]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "succession-and-legacy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why the settlement did not endure",
        "content": "Karim’s death in 1779 unleashed struggles among his relatives. Lotf Ali Khan, the last major Zand ruler, lost Shiraz after a rupture with the city’s leading official, Haji Ebrahim. Captured after further resistance, he was executed in 1794 on Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar’s orders. Qajar conquest ended Zand rule before the conqueror’s formal coronation in 1796. [s1]\n\nThe Zands also matter to cultural history. The Institute of Ismaili Studies identifies continuities between Zand art and later Qajar artistic forms. Their significance therefore extends beyond a short interval between larger dynasties: they helped transmit artistic practices into the next period. [s3]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "zand-transitions",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Formation, consolidation, and collapse",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"1750: Alliance installs Ismail III in Isfahan\"] -->|\"Rivalry within alliance\"| B[\"1751: Karim defeats Ali Mardan\"]\n  B -->|\"Consolidation\"| C[\"Karim governs as wakil\"]\n  C -->|\"Death in 1779\"| D[\"Contested Zand successions\"]\n  D -->|\"Internal conflict and Qajar advance\"| E[\"1792: Qajars enter Shiraz\"]\n  E -->|\"Final defeat\"| F[\"1794: Lotf Ali captured and executed\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram separates Karim’s consolidation from the succession conflicts and Qajar advances that dismantled Zand rule.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
